<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cleopatra Selene II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">40 BCE – c. 5 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Cleopatra Selene</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cleopatra Selene II was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra VII</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>, born in 40 BCE as the twin of Alexander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helios">Helios</a>. After her parents' deaths she was raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by Octavia and around 25 BCE married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba II</a>, becoming queen of Mauretania. She promoted Greek and Egyptian culture at the court of Caesarea, and her son Ptolemy succeeded Juba.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cleopatra-selene-ii.jpg" alt="Cleopatra Selene II" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hichem algerino · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cleopatra_Selene_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cleopatra Selene was raised and educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> as a Ptolemaic princess. Her second name means &quot;moon&quot; in Greek, matching that of her twin Alexander Helios, &quot;sun&quot;. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> acknowledged the twins in 37 BCE, and in 34 BCE, at the Donations of Alexandria, he bestowed kingdoms on the children before huge crowds. Selene was made ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, though she was too young to rule.</p><p>After the defeat at Actium in 31 BCE her parents sent the children away, but Roman soldiers captured them on the road to Thebes, and their parents killed themselves as Octavian invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Octavian paraded the twins in his triumph in golden chains so heavy that they could not walk in them, which drew sympathy from onlookers. Augustus gave them to his sister Octavia to be raised on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatine-hill">Palatine Hill</a>. Selene is the only known surviving member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic dynasty</a>, and her brothers are not recorded again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Octavia arranged her marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba II</a>, a learned prince raised in Octavian's household. It likely took place in 25 BCE, when Augustus made the couple co-rulers of the client kingdom of Mauretania, and they renamed their capital Caesarea, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherchell">Cherchell</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, in the emperor's honour. Selene is said to have had great influence on Juba's policies. She brought scholars and artists from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, introduced the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> to Caesarea and imported sculptures from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p>The kingdom exported fish, grapes, pearls, figs, grain, wooden furniture and a purple dye from shellfish to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Selene minted coins in her own name as Queen Cleopatra, and the couple also issued a joint coinage. Building at Caesarea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a> mixed Egyptian, Greek and Roman styles and included a lighthouse like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lighthouse-of-alexandria">Pharos of Alexandria</a> and a seafront palace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The couple had a son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-of-mauretania">Ptolemy of Mauretania</a>, born in 10 BCE and named for her dynasty and her younger brother, who succeeded Juba, and probably a daughter whose name has not survived. Selene's death is placed about 5 BCE, and the country became extremely wealthy during the couple's reign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cleopatra Selene II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Bocchus II bequeathed the kingdom to Rome, Augustus installed Juba II in 25 BCE, and Juba married <b>Cleopatra Selene</b>, daughter of Cleopatra VII.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-mauretania">Kingdom of Mauretania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherchell">Cherchell</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=-18" aria-label="Alexandria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Cleopatra Selene II"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4992 4898h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4992 4898h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=-18">What was here in 18 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
